.\" OpenBSD-style concise man page
.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt PRINTF 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm printf
.Nd format and print data
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm printf
.Ar format
.Op Ar argument ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility formats and prints its arguments under the control of the
.Ar format
string.
The
.Ar format
is a character string which contains three types of objects:
plain characters, which are simply copied to standard output;
character escape sequences, which are converted and copied to
standard output; and format specifications, each of which causes
printing of the next successive
.Ar argument .
.Pp
If there are more
.Ar argument Ns s
than format specifications, the format string is reused
as often as necessary to consume all arguments.
Missing arguments are treated as empty strings for
.Cm %s
and as zero for numeric conversions.
.Pp
The following escape sequences are recognized:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Li \ea
Alert (bell)
.It Li \eb
Backspace
.It Li \ef
Form feed
.It Li \en
New line
.It Li \er
Carriage return
.It Li \et
Horizontal tab
.It Li \ev
Vertical tab
.It Li \e\e
Backslash
.It Li \e0nnn
Character with octal value nnn (1-3 digits)
.It Li \exhh
Character with hex value hh (1-2 digits)
.El
.Pp
The following format specifications are supported:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Li %s
String
.It Li %b
String with backslash escape interpretation
.It Li %c
First character of argument
.It Li %d , %i
Signed decimal integer
.It Li %u
Unsigned decimal integer
.It Li %o
Unsigned octal integer
.It Li %x , %X
Unsigned hexadecimal integer (lowercase/uppercase)
.It Li %f
Floating-point number
.It Li %e , %E
Scientific notation (lowercase/uppercase)
.It Li %g , %G
Shorter of %f or %e (lowercase/uppercase)
.It Li %%
Literal percent sign
.El
.Pp
Format specifications may include optional flags, field width,
and precision:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl
Left-justify within the given field width.
.It Li +
Always show sign for signed conversions.
.It Li 0
Zero-pad numeric conversions.
.It Ar width
Minimum field width.
.It Li \&. Ns Ar precision
For strings, maximum characters to print.
For floats, digits after the decimal point.
.El
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl Fl help
Display help message and exit.
.It Fl Fl version
Display version information and exit.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std printf
.Sh EXAMPLES
Print a formatted string with a newline:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf "Hello, %s!\en" world
Hello, world!
.Ed
.Pp
Print a padded table:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf "%-10s %5d\en" Alice 30 Bob 25
Alice         30
Bob           25
.Ed
.Pp
Print a number in hex:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf "%#x\en" 255
0xff
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr echo 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility is compliant with the
IEEE Std 1003.1-2024 ("POSIX.1")
specification.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An "vibeutils implementation by Travis Cole"
